WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.200 --> 00:00:02.573
USER: I'm opening YouTube.

2
00:00:02.573 --> 00:00:04.700
USER: What happens behind the scenes?

3
00:00:05.667 --> 00:00:12.133
Every time you open a website, watch a video, or send a message, something interesting happens behind the screen.

4
00:00:12.467 --> 00:00:15.300
Your data travels across networks in the form of packets.

5
00:00:15.667 --> 00:00:18.500
But how do we know what's actually moving through our network?

6
00:00:24.200 --> 00:00:26.567
Think of a network packet as a digital envelope.

7
00:00:26.933 --> 00:00:34.100
It contains information about where it came from, where it's going, how it should be delivered, and the actual data being transmitted.

8
00:00:34.733 --> 00:00:36.800
Open it up, and you'll find layers.

9
00:00:37.133 --> 00:00:41.100
The Ethernet header carries the hardware addresses for this hop on the local network.

10
00:00:41.467 --> 00:00:44.433
The IP header holds the source and destination addresses.

11
00:00:44.800 --> 00:00:49.067
The TCP header adds port numbers, and keeps the conversation in order.

12
00:00:49.400 --> 00:00:51.330
And at the centre is the payload.

13
00:00:51.330 --> 00:00:52.967
The actual application data.

14
00:00:54.567 --> 00:00:56.997
ENGINEER: A firewall can see where a packet is going.

15
00:00:56.997 --> 00:00:58.467
ENGINEER: But not always what it is.

16
00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:03.100
A traditional firewall primarily evaluates packet headers and configured policies.

17
00:01:03.433 --> 00:01:06.191
Here, it sees a connection to port 443.

18
00:01:06.191 --> 00:01:08.733
Secure web traffic, but from which application?

19
00:01:09.400 --> 00:01:11.767
Our Deep Packet Inspection Engine goes further.

20
00:01:12.133 --> 00:01:19.200
It analyzes packet structure and supported application-layer information, to identify the traffic and apply intelligent filtering rules.

21
00:01:23.167 --> 00:01:27.422
ENGINEER: We capture traffic with a tool like Wireshark, and save it as a PCAP file.

22
00:01:27.422 --> 00:01:30.700
ENGINEER: The engine works on that recording, one packet at a time.

23
00:01:31.333 --> 00:01:43.333
Our DPI Engine takes captured network traffic as input, processes each packet, identifies supported applications, checks configured blocking rules, and produces filtered traffic, along with a detailed processing report.

24
00:01:48.767 --> 00:01:51.042
ENGINEER: Your browser generates network traffic.

25
00:01:51.042 --> 00:01:54.833
ENGINEER: Our DPI Engine analyzes the packets to understand the connection.

26
00:01:55.467 --> 00:01:57.767
Every packet enters through the PCAP Reader.

27
00:01:58.100 --> 00:02:05.200
It reads the file's global header, then each packet's record header, with its timestamp and length, followed by the raw packet bytes.

28
00:02:05.867 --> 00:02:08.600
The Packet Parser extracts the network information.

29
00:02:08.933 --> 00:02:11.022
MAC addresses from Ethernet.

30
00:02:11.022 --> 00:02:14.005
IP addresses and the protocol from IPv4.

31
00:02:14.005 --> 00:02:16.467
And port numbers from TCP or UDP.

32
00:02:17.100 --> 00:02:22.733
The engine then uses five important values, known as the Five-Tuple, to identify and track a connection.

33
00:02:23.067 --> 00:02:23.917
Source IP.

34
00:02:23.917 --> 00:02:25.194
Destination IP.

35
00:02:25.194 --> 00:02:26.214
Source port.

36
00:02:26.214 --> 00:02:27.661
Destination port.

37
00:02:27.661 --> 00:02:28.767
And protocol.

38
00:02:29.133 --> 00:02:32.333
Every packet with the same five values belongs to the same flow.

39
00:02:36.367 --> 00:02:41.256
Before a browser and a server exchange encrypted data, they perform a TLS handshake.

40
00:02:41.256 --> 00:02:43.933
The browser speaks first, with a Client Hello.

41
00:02:44.600 --> 00:02:53.500
Even though HTTPS encrypts application data, a conventional TLS Client Hello may expose the requested hostname, through the Server Name Indication extension.

42
00:02:53.833 --> 00:02:58.833
Our DPI Engine searches for this information, and maps recognized domains to application types.

43
00:02:59.600 --> 00:03:01.371
The engine never decrypts anything.

44
00:03:01.371 --> 00:03:03.800
Everything after the handshake stays unreadable.

45
00:03:04.167 --> 00:03:07.330
SNI extraction needs a complete, parsable Client Hello.

46
00:03:07.330 --> 00:03:12.333
And when the name is concealed by Encrypted Client Hello, there is no hostname to read.

47
00:03:12.667 --> 00:03:16.533
For unencrypted web traffic, the engine reads the HTTP Host header instead.

48
00:03:21.967 --> 00:03:26.800
Once the hostname is extracted, the engine compares it against known application signatures.

49
00:03:27.167 --> 00:03:31.800
This allows the system to classify recognizable traffic into application categories.

50
00:03:32.333 --> 00:03:35.240
Traffic it cannot recognize is simply labelled unknown.

51
00:03:35.240 --> 00:03:38.833
And the result is stored on the flow, for every packet that follows.

52
00:03:43.567 --> 00:03:48.900
ADMIN: Once the application is identified, we can apply the configured network policies.

53
00:03:49.533 --> 00:03:54.233
The engine evaluates traffic against configured IP, application, and domain rules.

54
00:03:55.200 --> 00:04:02.400
When a flow is identified as blocked, the engine marks that connection as blocked, and drops subsequent packets belonging to that flow.

55
00:04:03.033 --> 00:04:04.041
Notice the timing.

56
00:04:04.041 --> 00:04:08.969
The handshake packets were already forwarded, because the application was not known yet.

57
00:04:08.969 --> 00:04:11.433
Blocking begins once the flow is identified.

58
00:04:16.167 --> 00:04:21.200
To handle larger captures efficiently, the multithreaded version introduces parallel processing.

59
00:04:21.533 --> 00:04:26.833
A Reader thread pulls packets from the file, and Load Balancers distribute them across Fast Path threads.

60
00:04:27.500 --> 00:04:34.433
Consistent hashing ensures that packets belonging to the same connection reach the same processing thread, allowing the engine to maintain flow state.

61
00:04:34.767 --> 00:04:38.667
Hash the Five-Tuple, and the same connection always lands on the same thread.

62
00:04:39.433 --> 00:04:41.767
Between the stages sit thread-safe queues.

63
00:04:41.767 --> 00:04:45.100
A producer pushes packets in, and a consumer takes them out.

64
00:04:45.433 --> 00:04:50.367
A mutex guards the queue, and a condition variable wakes the consumer when work arrives.

65
00:04:50.733 --> 00:04:53.400
Finally, the Output Writer thread collects the results.

66
00:04:59.767 --> 00:05:03.833
After processing, the engine writes allowed packets into a filtered PCAP file.

67
00:05:04.200 --> 00:05:11.900
And it generates a report containing packet statistics, application breakdowns, detected domains, and thread-level processing information.

68
00:05:12.633 --> 00:05:16.733
ADMIN: Everything we forwarded, and everything we dropped, is accounted for.

69
00:05:26.367 --> 00:05:29.033
So, this is our Deep Packet Inspection Engine.

70
00:05:29.400 --> 00:05:40.333
It reads captured network traffic, understands packet structures, identifies supported applications, tracks connections, applies blocking rules, and produces filtered traffic with detailed reports.

71
00:05:41.267 --> 00:05:42.710
It's not just about seeing packets.

72
00:05:42.710 --> 00:05:45.267
It's about understanding the traffic moving through a network.

